Title: "Declares"
Id: "94516121-2dd0-4ec4-9a4e-b50a4ae45753"

Text: "```mlg-fragment
[CommandHeader]
Declares: <DeclarationStatement>
using?: <DeclarationStatement>+
when?: <ClauseUnion>+
expresses?: <ClauseUnion>
Requires?: <RequiresItemUnion>+
Enables?: <EnablesItemUnion>+
Documented?: <DocumentedItemUnion>+
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
Id?: <OpenText>
```

`Declares:` introduces a command by an `is` or specification statement — a
value, a statement, or a specification rather than a new type. The `expresses:`
section records the defining clause.

```mlg-fragment
[\\foo{s}]
Declares: x is \\bar{s}
Documented:
. called: \"foo\"
Id: \"23e4567e-e89b-42d3-a456-426614174000\"
```

A `Declares:` target symbol must be assigned exactly once and **must state its
type**, either with `... is <type>` or with a top-level build
`\\<type>@<value>`:

```mlg-fragment
Declares: X := \\set@{x_ : x_ is \\real}
```

A bare `X := {...}` is rejected even when the type could be inferred.

`using:` introduces auxiliary symbols. `when:` states requirements for
command-header parameters. `Requires:` and `Enables:` describe capabilities the
checker can use when resolving notation. `Documented:` must include at least one
of `called:` or `written:`. `Id:` is required for every top-level item;
`mlg check` adds it when missing.

A `Requires:` `definition:` item refers to a `Declares:` entry, so a command
whose definitional facts other items depend on is written with `Declares:`."
Id: "bf47cb67-b5e3-44de-add0-6b767a92aec8"
